## 2. @Bean

**Meaning:** Used to create an object managed by Spring.

```java
@Configuration
class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public Student student() {
        return new Student();
    }
}
```

**Meaning:** The `student()` object becomes a Spring Bean.

---

## 3. @Qualifier

**Meaning:** Used to select a specific Bean when multiple Beans have the same type.

```java
@Component("car")
class Car implements Vehicle {
}

@Component("bike")
class Bike implements Vehicle {
}

@Component
class Driver {

    @Autowired
    @Qualifier("car")
    Vehicle vehicle;
}
```

**Meaning:** Spring selects the `car` object specifically.

---

## 4. @Value

**Meaning:** Used to inject a value into a field.

```java
@Component
class Student {

    @Value("Vishwa")
    String name;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
```

**Output:**

```text
Vishwa
```

**Meaning:** The value `"Vishwa"` is assigned to `name`.

---

## 5. @DependsOn

**Meaning:** Specifies that one Bean must be created before another Bean.

```java
@Component("database")
class Database {
}

@Component
@DependsOn("database")
class Student {
}
```

**Meaning:** `database` is created before `Student`.

---

## 6. @Lazy

**Meaning:** Delays Bean creation until the Bean is actually needed.

```java
@Component
@Lazy
class Student {

    Student() {
        System.out.println("Student object created");
    }
}
```

**Meaning:** The `Student` object is not created when the application starts; it is created when required.

---

## 7. @Lookup

**Meaning:** Used to obtain a Bean instance through a lookup method, commonly when a singleton needs a prototype Bean.

```java
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
class Student {
}

@Component
class College {

    @Lookup
    public Student getStudent() {
        return null;
    }
}
```


Spring annotations reduce XML configuration and make dependency management and application configuration easier.
